INSERT INTO "SearchIndex" (id, "moduleId", "contentType", title, content, tags) VALUES
  ('search-cur-reset-promise-care', 'cur-reset-promise-care', 'module', 'The Promise + Ditch CARE', 'Turn See It. Own It. Elevate It. into four observable moves every teammate can use.
Outcomes: State the Ditch operating promise from memory.; Use CARE to choose the next useful action.; Explain how See It, Own It, and Elevate It map to the guest experience.
Our operating promise: Every guest leaves feeling seen, cared for, and genuinely glad they chose Ditch.
Ditch CARE: C - Catch the Cue: notice the guest, room, teammate, timing, and next need.; A - Act With Ownership: take the next useful action instead of pointing, waiting, or saying it is not yours.; R - Resolve + Recover: close the loop, communicate early, and repair misses with care.; E - End Strong: verify the result, thank the guest sincerely, and create a clear reason to return.
The operating language: See It -> Catch the cue before the need becomes a complaint.; Own It -> Act, resolve, or make a named handoff and verify it.; Elevate It -> Personalize appropriately and end the moment stronger than it began.
Cue-to-action sprint: For five common guest or teammate cues, name the CARE move and the first observable action within five seconds.. Proof: Names the cue; Chooses an owned action; Includes a closed loop; Avoids guessing or overpromising', ARRAY['curriculum', 'hospitality-reset', 'care', 'required', 'practical-required']::text[]),
  ('search-cur-reset-baseline-magic', 'cur-reset-baseline-magic', 'module', 'Baseline First. Magic Second.', 'Protect reliable fundamentals before adding personalized, one-size-fits-one hospitality.
Outcomes: Separate the hospitality baseline from an elevated magic moment.; Protect accuracy, communication, safety, and clean resets under pressure.; Create personalization without sacrificing another guest''s baseline.
The governing rule: A memorable gesture cannot compensate for an invisible arrival, inaccurate order, unsafe guess, silent delay, dirty station, or unfinished handoff. Reliability earns the right to personalize.
Baseline vs. magic: Immediate acknowledgment and a full greet as soon as guest care allows -> Use a remembered preference or occasion to shape the welcome; Accurate order, honest expectations, current product knowledge -> Guide the guest toward a choice that fits their exact mood; Early updates, specific check-backs, closed handoffs -> Remove a friction point before the guest notices it; Safe allergy and alcohol procedures; clean station and reset -> Create an appropriate personal detail after the baseline is protected
Guest journey baseline: Acknowledge immediately; a guest may wait during volume but should never feel invisible.; Learn the guest''s reason, energy, appetite, pace, and needs.; Guide with one or two useful questions and one confident recommendation.; Communicate delays, 86s, timing, mistakes, and changes before frustration builds.; Check back specifically about temperature, flavor, spice, timing, or the corrected item.; Process payment promptly, thank the guest sincerely, and acknowledge the departure.
Protect the baseline: A regular wants a long conversation while a new party arrives and a kitchen bell sounds. Prioritize the next three actions and preserve warmth with everyone.. Proof: Acknowledges the arrival; Answers or names the bell handoff; Pauses the regular naturally; Returns to close both loops', ARRAY['curriculum', 'hospitality-reset', 'baseline', 'guest-journey', 'required', 'practical-required']::text[]),
  ('search-cur-reset-whole-room', 'cur-reset-whole-room', 'module', 'Every Guest Belongs to All of Us', 'Build five-foot awareness, team hospitality, urgency, and clean handoffs without confusion or scorekeeping.
Outcomes: Use the five-foot mentality; Make a named handoff; Help the room without abandoning the assigned role
The ownership rule: If you see the need, acknowledge it. If you can solve it, solve it. If you cannot, make a named handoff and close the loop.
What beats low-priority work: Arrivals and waiting guests; Food in the pass, kitchen bells, and printed tickets; Empty drinks and dirty tables; Safety, spills, and allergy communication; A teammate approaching failure
Ditch language: That is not my table. -> I can help, or I will grab the right person and make sure they come over.; Can someone run this? -> Jordan, can you run this to 24? I will take 28.; I told them. -> I handed it to Alex, Alex confirmed it, and I checked that it was done.
Five-foot floor proof: During one shift, complete one unrequested teammate assist and repair three things simply because they entered your five feet.. Proof: Help actually reduces friction; Normal responsibilities remain covered; Handoffs are named and confirmed; Manager records specific evidence', ARRAY['curriculum', 'hospitality-reset', 'ownership', 'teamwork', 'required', 'practical-required']::text[]),
  ('search-cur-reset-passion-selling', 'cur-reset-passion-selling', 'module', 'Passion Selling: Guide, Do Not Push', 'Use observation and product knowledge to reduce uncertainty and help a guest choose what fits.
Outcomes: Use the five-step Passion Selling formula; Ask one useful preference question; Give a truthful sensory recommendation with a point of view
Observe > Ask > Interpret > Recommend > Describe: Observe: notice appetite, pace, occasion, flavor clues, and how much guidance the guest wants.; Ask: use one or two efficient questions - bright or rich, mild or spicy, citrus or smoky, spirit-forward or zero proof.; Interpret: turn the answer into direction instead of repeating the whole menu.; Recommend: take a position - ''For what you described, I would go with...''.; Describe: use truthful texture, flavor, contrast, fit, and a reason to care.
From order taking to guidance: Everything is good. -> Tell me what sounds better tonight - bright and lighter or rich and comforting?; Do you want an appetizer? -> Before we get into mains, there are two shareables I would not want you to miss.; Would you like another drink? -> I can keep you in this same lane or take you somewhere different - what sounds good?
Current practice set: Use current descriptions and verified flavor lanes. Do not add ingredients, allergy claims, or availability from memory.. Items: food-yuca-poppers, food-taco-jerk-shrimp, food-lobster-roll, food-zuma-beach-bowl, drink-hang-10, drink-smoke-on-the-bay, drink-pineapple-basil-smash, drink-watermelon-cooler
The twenty-second challenge: Ask one preference question, recommend one current item, explain why it fits, and set one honest expectation in twenty seconds.. Proof: Learns a real preference; Takes a clear position; Uses truthful sensory language; Avoids pressure and ingredient dumping; Uses only current source facts', ARRAY['curriculum', 'hospitality-reset', 'passion-selling', 'menu', 'required', 'practical-required']::text[]),
  ('search-cur-reset-source-safety', 'cur-reset-source-safety', 'module', 'Never Guess: Source Discipline + Safety', 'Know where current truth lives and stop before an uncertain allergy, recipe, beer, or policy answer becomes guest risk.
Outcomes: Use approved sources instead of memory; Execute the allergy communication chain; Identify verification-required and rotating menu content
Accuracy is hospitality: If you are unsure, say: ''Let me confirm that so I give you the right answer.'' Never guess on allergies, alcohol service, a recipe, or an unfamiliar current product.
Allergy communication chain: Acknowledge the concern without promising an outcome; Mark it using the current POS procedure; Communicate it verbally; Involve the manager; Allow the kitchen to confirm what can be prepared safely; Confirm the approved delivery/handoff
Verification-required content [verification-required]: Do not certify allergy specifics from menu descriptions. Do not train Ancho Average, Sunburnt Summer, Frozen Paloma, Da Painkiller, Island Rum Punch, or Hibiscus Refresher until the recipe lock is approved. Pink salt, wine-pour, and par conflicts also remain manager verification items.
Rotating beer [rotating]: Beer selections change by location and date. Demonstrate how to verify the live POS/posted list; do not memorize a historic fixed list as permanent truth.', ARRAY['curriculum', 'hospitality-reset', 'safety', 'allergy', 'source-control', 'required', 'practical-required']::text[]),
  ('search-cur-reset-last', 'cur-reset-last', 'module', 'L.A.S.T. Guest Recovery', 'Repair trust, own the problem, follow up personally, and improve the system behind the miss.
Outcomes: Use Listen, Apologize, Solve, Think; Keep same-team language; Follow up and identify the system lesson
L.A.S.T.: Listen: hear the complete concern and summarize what the guest actually cares about.; Apologize: acknowledge the experience without excuses, blame, or a defensive ''but''.; Solve: take meaningful action; own the issue until it is solved or physically and verbally transferred.; Think: after the guest is cared for, capture what allowed the miss and how the system should improve.
Same-team recovery language: The kitchen messed it up. -> We missed that. I am getting it corrected right now.; We are slammed. -> Thank you for waiting. I have you, and I am getting an update now.; That is our policy. -> Here is what I can do. Let me confirm the best next step with my manager.
A human recovery: You are right to be frustrated.; Let me take care of this with you.; I will come back personally to make sure we got it right.
Recovery reps: Run a cold item, a long wait, and a correctly made item the guest simply dislikes.. Proof: Listens without interruption; No blame or excuses; Solution fits the concern and role boundary; Follows up; Names a system lesson', ARRAY['curriculum', 'hospitality-reset', 'recovery', 'last', 'required', 'practical-required']::text[]),
  ('search-cur-reset-accountability', 'cur-reset-accountability', 'module', 'How We Work Here: Professionalism + Accountability', 'Make readiness, communication, coachability, checklists, resets, and follow-through observable.
Outcomes: Describe the ready-to-work standard; Receive coaching with Heard and act; Explain how performance and schedules connect
Observable operating standards: Arrive on time, in uniform, with coffee, food, and personal tasks handled.; Keep phones away and out of guest view unless the MOD approves a specific use.; Use professional language: no profanity, gossip, arguing, sexual or inappropriate conversation, panic, blame, or personal drama.; Check 7shifts before work; a read receipt means the instruction was received and must be followed.; Complete and honestly verify opening, handoff, closing, weekly cleaning, PAR, and sidework sheets.; Count first; cover, label on tape, date, FIFO, and return product to its assigned location.; Leave the complete station ready for the next shift.
Receive coaching cleanly: Heard.; Make the immediate correction.; If needed after the reset: ''Fixed. Can you show me what you want next time so I hit it consistently?''
What accountability means: Great behavior is recognized specifically. Misses are redirected in real time. Recurring patterns, false sign-offs, ignored direction, or repeated failure to close loops are documented and coached. Schedules reflect business needs and demonstrated reliability, hospitality, pace, teamwork, knowledge, follow-through, and coachability.
One-week commitment: Choose one observable behavior to improve for seven days and capture floor evidence every shift.. Proof: Behavior is specific; Manager can observe it; Employee receives coaching without a courtroom; Evidence shows whether it changed', ARRAY['curriculum', 'hospitality-reset', 'accountability', 'professionalism', 'required', 'practical-required']::text[]),
  ('search-cur-reset-live-certification', 'cur-reset-live-certification', 'module', 'Hospitality Reset: Live Floor Certification', 'Prove the reset through live behavior, not attendance or vocabulary.
Outcomes: Execute the baseline during live service; Create one appropriate magic moment; Receive coaching and improve on a second take
Certification proof: Immediate acknowledgment; Guest discovery and confident guidance; Current-source discipline; Whole-room ownership and clean handoffs; Early communication and specific check-back; Professional coaching response; Strong close
Observed service block: A qualified trainer observes a realistic service block and records specific evidence for every baseline behavior plus one personalized moment.. Proof: No critical safety or integrity miss; Baseline appears consistently; Magic does not steal from another guest; One coached miss improves on the second take; Trainer records evidence, not personality labels', ARRAY['curriculum', 'hospitality-reset', 'certification', 'live-observation', 'required', 'practical-required']::text[]),
  ('search-cur-leadership-week-0', 'cur-leadership-week-0', 'module', 'Week 0 - Culture Calibration', 'Name the culture that actually exists before trying to improve it.
Outcomes: Score observable behavior rather than intentions, personalities, or the restaurant''s best night.; Teach the standard to another leader; Produce live floor evidence
Why it matters: Aspirational language cannot repair tolerated behavior. Honest calibration gives leadership one shared starting point.
The standard: Use specific floor evidence, agree on the largest gaps, and choose a small number of behaviors to stop tolerating immediately.
What great looks like: Scores include observed examples; Leaders name three priority gaps; One behavior to stop and one behavior to celebrate are selected; The first reset has an owner
Leader language: What behavior currently survives here?; What would a new employee copy after three shifts?
Run the drill: Compare Culture Audit scores. For every score that differs by more than two points, each leader provides one observed example.. Proof: Completed audit; Three priority gaps; One behavior to stop; One behavior to celebrate; Leadership agreement on first reset
On the floor: Observe one full shift across standards, accountability, hospitality, team hospitality, ownership, selling, recovery, systems, coaching, and training.. Proof: Completed audit; Three priority gaps; One behavior to stop; One behavior to celebrate; Leadership agreement on first reset
Watch for: Inflated scores; Blame; Vague labels such as bad attitude; Treating a system failure as a character flaw', ARRAY['curriculum', 'leadership-os', 'week-0', 'leadership-lab', 'required', 'practical-required']::text[]),
  ('search-cur-leadership-week-1', 'cur-leadership-week-1', 'module', 'Week 1 - Leadership Identity', 'Make leadership behavior the clearest training tool in the building.
Outcomes: Model before mandating, inspect what you expect, coach what you inspect, and reinforce what improves.; Teach the standard to another leader; Produce live floor evidence
Why it matters: The team studies what leaders model, ignore, reinforce, and repeat. A leader is always training.
The standard: Be visible, calm, willing to help, quick to own misses, and consistent when the room gets busy.
What great looks like: Leader demonstrates before demanding; Misses are inspected and coached; Improvement is recognized specifically; Leader owns a contradiction quickly
Leader language: Watch me once. Run it with me once. Now show me the standard without me.; What did the team learn from watching me in that moment?
Run the drill: Replay a leader walking past a miss, then replay it with modeling, coaching, and specific recognition.. Proof: Seven completed reflections; One live observation; Evidence of model, inspect, coach, reinforce
On the floor: For seven shifts, capture one moment when leadership behavior reinforced or contradicted the culture.. Proof: Seven completed reflections; One live observation; Evidence of model, inspect, coach, reinforce
Watch for: Demanding standards the leader does not follow; Correction without demonstration; Administrative hiding; Vague recognition', ARRAY['curriculum', 'leadership-os', 'week-1', 'leadership-lab', 'required', 'practical-required']::text[]),
  ('search-cur-leadership-week-2', 'cur-leadership-week-2', 'module', 'Week 2 - Standards Without Ego', 'Create fast, neutral accountability through the 10 for 10 reset and the no-ego response.
Outcomes: Anyone may protect the standard. The first response to correction is Heard, followed by action.; Teach the standard to another leader; Produce live floor evidence
Why it matters: Small disorder compounds. Emotional correction teaches defense instead of protecting the standard.
The standard: Notice drift early, call a neutral reset, join the work, verify completion, recognize the reset, and move on without theater.
What great looks like: 10 for 10 lasts roughly 30 seconds; Anyone can call it; Leader participates; Completion is verified and released
Leader language: 10 for 10, team. Thirty-second reset.; Beautiful - that is our standard. Let''s go.; Heard. Fixed. Can you show me what you want next time?
Run the drill: Create five minor station misses. Identify drift, call the reset, participate, recognize completion, and release the team.. Proof: Calls and executes a live reset; Receives a reset with Heard; Coaches without creating defensiveness
On the floor: Use 10 for 10 only when the environment is drifting and verify that the reset is actually complete.. Proof: Calls and executes a live reset; Receives a reset with Heard; Coaches without creating defensiveness
Watch for: Using the phrase as punishment; Calling it without helping; Public embarrassment; Sarcasm; Courtroom explanations', ARRAY['curriculum', 'leadership-os', 'week-2', 'leadership-lab', 'required', 'practical-required']::text[]),
  ('search-cur-leadership-week-3', 'cur-leadership-week-3', 'module', 'Week 3 - Unconditional Hospitality', 'Turn awareness into action for guests and coworkers regardless of mood or pressure.
Outcomes: Notice what a person needs, make it easier, personalize where possible, and act before they have to ask.; Teach the standard to another leader; Produce live floor evidence
Why it matters: Hospitality is felt in small moments of anticipation. When it depends on an easy guest or shift, it is not a standard.
The standard: Read the room, remove friction, match the guest''s preferred pace, and give teammates unrequested support.
What great looks like: Reads quiet and verbal cues; Acts before being asked; Matches pace without disappearing; Team help is useful and unscored
Leader language: What can I make better within the next five feet?; What can we solve before they ask?
Run the drill: For five guest scenarios, identify the need, friction, personal detail, and anticipatory action.. Proof: Three live anticipation examples; At least one coworker example; Trainer records the cue and action
On the floor: Log three anticipated needs, two coworker hospitality moments, and one personalized guest experience.. Proof: Three live anticipation examples; At least one coworker example; Trainer records the cue and action
Watch for: Robotic scripts; Over-talking; Helping only favorite coworkers; Missing quiet signals; Confusing speed with care', ARRAY['curriculum', 'leadership-os', 'week-3', 'leadership-lab', 'required', 'practical-required']::text[]),
  ('search-cur-leadership-week-4', 'cur-leadership-week-4', 'module', 'Week 4 - Passion Selling', 'Teach confident, personal recommendations that increase guest value without pressure.
Outcomes: Observe, ask, interpret, recommend, and describe; take a position based on the guest rather than reciting the menu.; Teach the standard to another leader; Produce live floor evidence
Why it matters: Guests gain value when knowledgeable employees reduce uncertainty. Revenue follows trust, relevance, and genuine enthusiasm.
The standard: Ask one useful preference question, recommend with confidence, use contrast, describe the experience, and offer a natural pairing.
What great looks like: Recommendation fits the clue; Description is sensory and truthful; Contrast sets expectations; No ingredient dump or automatic add-on
Leader language: For what you just described, I would absolutely send you toward...; Are you leaning brighter and lighter or richer and more comforting?
Run the drill: Sell one current item without good, delicious, or amazing and without listing ingredients; explain who would love it and who might not.. Proof: Trains one teammate; Three successful role plays; One observed live recommendation; Uses current menu facts
On the floor: Make three preference-based recommendations and record the question, recommendation, and guest response.. Proof: Trains one teammate; Three successful role plays; One observed live recommendation; Uses current menu facts
Watch for: Everything is good; Ingredient dumping; Fake enthusiasm; Automatic add-ons; Recommending before learning', ARRAY['curriculum', 'leadership-os', 'week-4', 'leadership-lab', 'required', 'practical-required']::text[]),
  ('search-cur-leadership-week-5', 'cur-leadership-week-5', 'module', 'Week 5 - Guest Recovery', 'Use L.A.S.T. to care for the guest and improve the system behind the miss.
Outcomes: Listen, Apologize, Solve, Think; own the problem until it is solved or physically and verbally transferred.; Teach the standard to another leader; Produce live floor evidence
Why it matters: A comp may correct a check, but recovery has to repair trust. The response can become more memorable than the miss.
The standard: Listen without defense, acknowledge the experience, offer a meaningful solution, follow up personally, and capture the cause.
What great looks like: No excuses or blame; Solution fits the concern; Guest receives a specific follow-up; System lesson has an owner
Leader language: You are right to be frustrated.; Let me take care of this with you.; I will come back to make sure we got it right.
Run the drill: Run recovery scenarios and score listening, ownership, solution quality, same-team language, follow-up, and system thinking.. Proof: Three simulated recoveries; One live or case-based recovery; Follow-up; System improvement
On the floor: Use the recovery log for every meaningful recovery and complete Think after the guest is cared for.. Proof: Three simulated recoveries; One live or case-based recovery; Follow-up; System improvement
Watch for: Excuses; Department blame; Policy shields; Arguing facts; Disappearing after the solution; Compensation without care', ARRAY['curriculum', 'leadership-os', 'week-5', 'leadership-lab', 'required', 'practical-required']::text[]),
  ('search-cur-leadership-week-6', 'cur-leadership-week-6', 'module', 'Week 6 - Accountability + Coaching', 'Correct observable behavior without ego, labels, humiliation, or delayed ambiguity.
Outcomes: Use observation, standard, impact, reset, and confirmation to make the next rep more likely to succeed.; Teach the standard to another leader; Produce live floor evidence
Why it matters: Clear, timely behavioral coaching protects the guest and gives the employee a usable path forward.
The standard: Coach facts rather than identity; diagnose clarity, skill, system, environment, and willingness before choosing the response.
What great looks like: Correction is timely and specific; Impact is connected to guest/team/safety; One reset behavior is named; Second-take evidence is captured
Leader language: Here is what I observed...; Our standard is...; Why that matters is...; What I need from you now is...; We are aligned?
Run the drill: Convert five vague labels into observable coaching statements, then deliver a 60-second coach and require a second take.. Proof: Behavioral observation; Correct standard; One clear reset; Second-take improvement; Appropriate privacy/urgency decision
On the floor: Use one timely 60-second coach and one Keep / Start / Stop debrief during a real shift.. Proof: Behavioral observation; Correct standard; One clear reset; Second-take improvement; Appropriate privacy/urgency decision
Watch for: Always and never; Personality labels; Public humiliation; Saving everything for later; Retraining a willingness problem', ARRAY['curriculum', 'leadership-os', 'week-6', 'leadership-lab', 'required', 'practical-required']::text[]),
  ('search-cur-leadership-week-7', 'cur-leadership-week-7', 'module', 'Week 7 - 80/20 Resourcefulness', 'Develop people who think, act, and bring recommendations upward.
Outcomes: Clarify facts, identify what is controllable, create options, take the next-best action, and recommend a move.; Teach the standard to another leader; Produce live floor evidence
Why it matters: Automatic escalation creates helplessness and leader bottlenecks. Resourcefulness separates the real constraint from the story around it.
The standard: Raise problems early with actions already taken, available options, and a recommendation; never hide safety or urgency.
What great looks like: Facts are separated from emotion; Controllables are named; Three options are considered; A recommendation is brought upward
Leader language: What have you tried?; What can you control?; What is the next-best move?; What are you recommending?
Run the drill: Give a common roadblock with one fixed constraint; produce three controllable actions and a recommendation in 60 seconds.. Proof: One employee coached through the full 80/20 worksheet; Sound recommendation; Appropriate action and escalation
On the floor: When a problem is escalated, coach the thinking before supplying the answer unless guest safety or urgency requires direction.. Proof: One employee coached through the full 80/20 worksheet; Sound recommendation; Appropriate action and escalation
Watch for: Hiding problems; Reckless improvisation; Learned helplessness; Leaders answering too quickly; Withholding support', ARRAY['curriculum', 'leadership-os', 'week-7', 'leadership-lab', 'required', 'practical-required']::text[]),
  ('search-cur-leadership-week-8', 'cur-leadership-week-8', 'module', 'Week 8 - Systems + Predictability', 'Convert repeated problems into owned, visible, testable systems.
Outcomes: Define the standard, owner, trigger, method, verification, and recovery for every critical system.; Teach the standard to another leader; Produce live floor evidence
Why it matters: If the same capable people fail the same process, another reminder is not a solution. Mature operations learn from repetition.
The standard: Build processes that survive a manager''s absence, make completion visible, and improve after breakdown.
What great looks like: One named owner; A clear trigger; Method and resources are defined; Verification is visible; Recovery path exists
Leader language: What system is allowing this result?; Who owns it, what triggers it, and how do we verify completion?
Run the drill: Build a six-part system for one recurring problem, hand it to another leader, and test it without extra explanation.. Proof: One real system built; Named owner and verification; Test completed; Revision based on evidence
On the floor: Capture one repeated failure, diagnose person versus system, and test one improvement during the week.. Proof: One real system built; Named owner and verification; Test completed; Revision based on evidence
Watch for: Everyone owns it; No trigger; Hidden completion; Missing resources; Constant exceptions; Systems living only in a manager''s head', ARRAY['curriculum', 'leadership-os', 'week-8', 'leadership-lab', 'required', 'practical-required']::text[]),
  ('search-cur-mastermind-unconditional-hospitality', 'cur-mastermind-unconditional-hospitality', 'module', 'Cycle 1 - Unconditional Hospitality', 'Awareness, anticipation, and care. Ten shifts, one five-minute behavior rep per shift.
Outcomes: Run all ten pre-shift sessions in sequence.; Observe and coach each day''s behavior during live service.; Repeat a day when behavior has not transferred to the floor.
Cycle standard: Name the behavior in pre-shift, observe it during service, coach or reinforce it live, and close the loop after the shift.
Day 1: What Hospitality Actually Means: Intention, not generic niceness. Niceness is a pleasant attitude. Hospitality notices something specific and improves another person''s experience because of it. Example: A warm welcome becomes hospitality when the teammate also notices an accessibility need, makes space, and lets the guest settle.
Five-minute drill - What Hospitality Actually Means: What is the difference between being nice and creating hospitality? Name one polite action and upgrade it into an intentional hospitality action.. Proof: Specific clues noticed; Action is personal rather than performative
Today''s floor challenge: Make one guest or coworker feel specifically seen.. Proof: Specific clues noticed; Action is personal rather than performative; Close with: Who felt specifically seen because of something we did?
Day 2: Anticipation: Solve the next need before the ask. Anticipation reads timing, body language, table context, and the predictable next need; it is not mind reading. Example: A family organizing bags while food arrives gets extra space and napkins before the first spill.
Five-minute drill - Anticipation: What can we solve tonight before someone asks? Call out three guest clues; name the most useful next action for each.. Proof: Cue seen but ignored; Help arrives at a useful time
Today''s floor challenge: Create one anticipated-need moment in every section.. Proof: Cue seen but ignored; Help arrives at a useful time; Close with: What did we solve before the guest had to ask?
Day 3: Coworker Hospitality: Give the team the care we promise guests. Hospitality moves in every direction. Useful, unrequested help protects the guest without creating debt or scorekeeping. Example: Run another section''s food before it dies in the window.
Five-minute drill - Coworker Hospitality: Would we tolerate ourselves treating a guest the way we sometimes treat each other? Name one unrequested assist your position can give another position.. Proof: No sarcasm or scorekeeping; Employee still owns normal responsibilities
Today''s floor challenge: Complete one unrequested assist that actually helps.. Proof: No sarcasm or scorekeeping; Employee still owns normal responsibilities; Close with: Who made someone else''s shift easier, and how?
Day 4: The Five-Foot Mentality: Own the condition around you. Your title does not shrink your awareness. Improve what is unsafe, confusing, dirty, delayed, or emotionally unattended in your immediate environment. Example: A leader heading to the office fixes the crooked chair, empty glass, and confused guest that enter their five feet.
Five-minute drill - The Five-Foot Mentality: What problems exist because people walk past them? Scan the room for ten seconds and name the first useful improvement.. Proof: Repeated walk-bys; Problems named without action or transfer
Today''s floor challenge: Fix or connect every obvious issue that enters your five feet.. Proof: Repeated walk-bys; Problems named without action or transfer; Close with: What did you fix simply because it entered your five feet?
Day 5: Names: Use recognition naturally. A name tells someone they are not interchangeable. Use it naturally and accurately; recognition is not a forced script. Example: Jordan, can you run this to 24? Thank you - instead of a vague call to the room.
Five-minute drill - Names: What happens when somebody remembers your name? Learn one useful detail from someone whose name or role you know least.. Proof: Warmth and accuracy; Names are not used only for correction
Today''s floor challenge: Learn and naturally use two names you do not normally use.. Proof: Warmth and accuracy; Names are not used only for correction; Close with: Whose name did you learn or use differently?
Day 6: Reading the Guest: Match the style of service to the table. Consistent care does not require identical delivery. Read verbal cues, eye contact, pace, occasion, and engagement. Example: A quiet conversation may need precise, low-interruption service; a celebratory group may welcome energy and guidance.
Five-minute drill - Reading the Guest: Does every table want the same style of service? Act out two table styles; choose an opening approach and explain the cue.. Proof: Over-talking or disappearing; Quiet guests still receive attention
Today''s floor challenge: Identify each table''s preferred service style and match it.. Proof: Over-talking or disappearing; Quiet guests still receive attention; Close with: Which table needed a different version of you?
Day 7: Removing Friction: Eliminate tiny inconveniences. Friction makes the guest or team work harder: missing items, late information, repeated questions, clutter, or awkward timing. Example: A guest with a deadline receives realistic menu guidance, kitchen communication, and a prepared check before anxiety builds.
Five-minute drill - Removing Friction: What small inconvenience can we eliminate tonight? Name one friction point at arrival, ordering, dining, payment, and departure; choose one to remove.. Proof: Recurring confusion; Workarounds that should become systems
Today''s floor challenge: Remove one friction point and tell the manager what changed.. Proof: Recurring confusion; Workarounds that should become systems; Close with: What became easier because we noticed it?
Day 8: Pace: Use timing as a form of care. Observe before approaching. Good pace balances attentiveness with space and anticipates the table''s next transition. Example: Let the guest take two bites before a specific check-back while a correction is still possible.
Five-minute drill - Pace: Can great hospitality feel bad if the timing is wrong? Choose approach now, wait, or prepare silently for three table moments.. Proof: Autopilot check-backs; Hovering or long disappearances
Today''s floor challenge: Pause and read before every non-routine approach.. Proof: Autopilot check-backs; Hovering or long disappearances; Close with: Where did better timing improve an interaction?
Day 9: Personalization: Use the clue already offered. Personalization uses a shared occasion, preference, time constraint, or prior visit to shape one appropriate part of the experience. Example: A guest who loved a bright, not-too-sweet margarita receives a relevant current recommendation instead of the whole menu again.
Five-minute drill - Personalization: What clue has the guest already given us? Use one guest clue to create a natural follow-through that does not feel invasive.. Proof: Relevant and restrained; Does not feel like surveillance or a sales trick
Today''s floor challenge: Use one real guest detail to personalize an experience.. Proof: Relevant and restrained; Does not feel like surveillance or a sales trick; Close with: What detail made an experience feel less generic?
Day 10: Hospitality Story: Turn great moments into culture. Specific recognition names the clue, the action, and why it mattered so the whole room can repeat it. Example: Not ''Sam crushed it''; name the ferry-time clue, the pacing action, and the stress it removed.
Five-minute drill - Hospitality Story: What did someone do during this cycle that deserves to be copied? Tell one 30-second story: what was noticed, what was done, and why it mattered.. Proof: Specific behavior; Missing details are requested
Today''s floor challenge: Create one final hospitality moment worth telling tomorrow.. Proof: Specific behavior; Missing details are requested; Close with: Which behavior should become part of every shift?', ARRAY['curriculum', 'daily-mastermind', 'cycle-1', 'leadership', 'floor-training', 'practical-required']::text[]),
  ('search-cur-mastermind-passion-selling', 'cur-mastermind-passion-selling', 'module', 'Cycle 2 - Passion Selling', 'Confident, personal recommendations. Ten shifts, one five-minute behavior rep per shift.
Outcomes: Run all ten pre-shift sessions in sequence.; Observe and coach each day''s behavior during live service.; Repeat a day when behavior has not transferred to the floor.
Cycle standard: Use current sources and the full Observe > Ask > Interpret > Recommend > Describe formula without pressure or invention.
Day 1: Beyond ''It''s Good'': Describe why an item matters. Generic praise gives no decision help. Explain what makes the item distinctive, who it fits, and the experience to expect. Example: Describe Yuca Poppers through the crisp shell, soft savory center, bright guacamole, and easy-to-share fit.
Five-minute drill - Beyond ''It''s Good'': Can you recommend a favorite without good, amazing, or delicious? Give a 20-second current-menu recommendation without generic praise or an ingredient dump.. Proof: Truth and specificity; Description helps the decision
Today''s floor challenge: Use one sensory detail and a point of view with a guest.. Proof: Truth and specificity; Description helps the decision; Close with: Which description made a guest more confident?
Day 2: Sell Through Contrast: Set expectations with accurate contrast. Truthful contrast makes style and fit understandable without insulting the item. Example: Hang 10 is bright, citrus-forward, and clean rather than a sugary frozen margarita.
Five-minute drill - Sell Through Contrast: What becomes clearer when you explain what an item is not? Describe one food and one drink using a truthful contrast.. Proof: Contrast stays positive; No false or stale claims
Today''s floor challenge: Use one contrast statement in a real recommendation.. Proof: Contrast stays positive; No false or stale claims; Close with: What expectation did contrast help set?
Day 3: Ask Before Recommending: Earn the right to guide. One efficient preference question prevents menu dumping and makes the recommendation relevant. Example: Are you leaning bright and refreshing or smoky and spicy?
Five-minute drill - Ask Before Recommending: What is the smallest useful question you can ask? A guest gives a vague request; ask one question before choosing a direction.. Proof: Question is efficient; Answer actually shapes the choice
Today''s floor challenge: Ask a useful preference question before three recommendations.. Proof: Question is efficient; Answer actually shapes the choice; Close with: Which question produced the best recommendation?
Day 4: Open the Experience: Create curiosity before taking orders. A relevant opening direction helps the table picture the experience; it is not a memorized appetizer pitch. Example: For a first-time sharing table, explain street-taco size and offer one current shareable before mains.
Five-minute drill - Open the Experience: How can an opening recommendation feel like care instead of an add-on? Create two natural openings for different guest profiles.. Proof: No monologue; Direction fits the table
Today''s floor challenge: Use one guided opening recommendation before taking the order.. Proof: No monologue; Direction fits the table; Close with: Did the opening create curiosity or just add cost?
Day 5: Take a Position: Reduce uncertainty with confidence. After learning the preference, choose a direction and explain why. Confidence should come from fit and current knowledge. Example: For what you described, I would choose the Jerk Shrimp Tacos.
Five-minute drill - Take a Position: Why does ''either one is good'' fail the guest? Respond to two preferences with one recommendation and one reason.. Proof: Recommendation fits; No promise that the guest must love it
Today''s floor challenge: Use ''For what you described...'' in one real interaction.. Proof: Recommendation fits; No promise that the guest must love it; Close with: Where did taking a position make the decision easier?
Day 6: Texture Sells: Make the bite understandable. Texture often gives more useful information than a list of ingredients. Example: Yuca Poppers: crisp outside, soft and savory in the center, with creamy guacamole and bright sauces.
Five-minute drill - Texture Sells: Which texture word helps someone picture the food? Describe one current item through texture in one sentence.. Proof: Sensory accuracy; Concise, current description
Today''s floor challenge: Use one texture-led description with a guest.. Proof: Sensory accuracy; Concise, current description; Close with: Which texture word helped a guest decide?
Day 7: Describe the Experience: Sell the feeling, not the inventory. Translate the build into the way it eats or drinks: bright, filling, crisp, smoky, shareable, tropical, or refreshing. Example: The Zuma Beach Bowl eats fresh and filling at the same time, with vegetables, quinoa, and crunch.
Five-minute drill - Describe the Experience: Can you describe an item without listing every ingredient? Describe one food without naming an ingredient.. Proof: No empty adjectives; No overpromise
Today''s floor challenge: Give one experience-led description.. Proof: No empty adjectives; No overpromise; Close with: What experience did you help a guest picture?
Day 8: Pair With Purpose: Explain why two choices work together. A pairing needs a reason connected to flavor, texture, heat, richness, or refreshment. Example: Pineapple Basil Smash adds herbal pineapple lift alongside Jerk Shrimp Tacos; Hang 10''s lime and salt cut through Yuca Poppers.
Five-minute drill - Pair With Purpose: What makes a pairing useful rather than automatic? Choose one verified food-and-drink pairing and explain the reason.. Proof: Current, approved items only; Reason is specific and restrained
Today''s floor challenge: Offer one reasoned pairing after learning the guest''s preference.. Proof: Current, approved items only; Reason is specific and restrained; Close with: Which pairing genuinely improved an order?
Day 9: When They Are Not Hungry: Right-size the experience. Respect appetite while still helping. Recommend the right amount, not the largest amount. Example: If you want one thing in the middle, I would choose Yuca Poppers; if you want lighter, mix two tacos instead.
Five-minute drill - When They Are Not Hungry: How do we guide without pushing when appetite is small? Role-play ''We are not that hungry'' with one right-sized recommendation.. Proof: Respects appetite; No oversized automatic recommendation
Today''s floor challenge: Make one right-sized recommendation without pressure.. Proof: Respects appetite; No oversized automatic recommendation; Close with: Did we respect appetite while still being useful?
Day 10: Twenty-Second Challenge: Combine the full formula. Use Observe, Ask, Interpret, Recommend, Describe naturally and efficiently. Example: You said bright and not sweet, so I would go Hang 10. It is citrus-forward, clean, and the salt rim keeps it lively.
Five-minute drill - Twenty-Second Challenge: Can you learn, recommend, and describe in twenty seconds? Deliver a 20-second recommendation for a surprise guest profile.. Proof: Preference learned; Current facts; Clear point of view; Human pace
Today''s floor challenge: Deliver your cleanest complete recommendation of the cycle.. Proof: Preference learned; Current facts; Clear point of view; Human pace; Close with: Which part of the formula now feels natural?', ARRAY['curriculum', 'daily-mastermind', 'cycle-2', 'leadership', 'floor-training', 'practical-required']::text[]),
  ('search-cur-mastermind-last-recovery', 'cur-mastermind-last-recovery', 'module', 'Cycle 3 - L.A.S.T. Recovery', 'Guest recovery and system learning. Ten shifts, one five-minute behavior rep per shift.
Outcomes: Run all ten pre-shift sessions in sequence.; Observe and coach each day''s behavior during live service.; Repeat a day when behavior has not transferred to the floor.
Cycle standard: Care for the guest first, own the problem through follow-up, then improve the system behind the miss.
Day 1: Listen Without Interrupting: Hear the concern beneath the complaint. Do not prepare a defense while the guest talks. Listen for impact, emotion, expectation, and the outcome they need. Example: A complaint about a long wait may also be about feeling forgotten and unable to plan.
Five-minute drill - Listen Without Interrupting: What does the guest care about beyond the first sentence? One person speaks for 30 seconds; the listener summarizes without rebuttal.. Proof: No interruption; Summary captures more than the literal words
Today''s floor challenge: Summarize the concern before proposing a solution.. Proof: No interruption; Summary captures more than the literal words; Close with: What did the guest actually care about?
Day 2: Apologize Without Excuses: Acknowledge before explaining. Apologize for the experience and accept responsibility to care for it; context can wait. Example: You should not have waited that long without an update. I am getting one now.
Five-minute drill - Apologize Without Excuses: What makes an apology lower the temperature? Rewrite three excuse-first apologies into direct acknowledgment.. Proof: No ''but''; Acknowledgment is specific
Today''s floor challenge: Give any necessary apology before explanation.. Proof: No ''but''; Acknowledgment is specific; Close with: Did the apology lower the temperature?
Day 3: Replace ''But'': Stay on the guest''s team. But often cancels ownership. Replace it with what you can do, what you will verify, and when you will return. Example: Instead of ''I am sorry, but that is policy,'' say ''Here is what I can do; let me confirm the next step.''
Five-minute drill - Replace ''But'': What word quietly turns an apology into a debate? Rapid-fire replace five recovery ''buts''.. Proof: No blame or policy shield; Action remains honest
Today''s floor challenge: Catch and replace every recovery ''but''.. Proof: No blame or policy shield; Action remains honest; Close with: Which phrase kept us on the same team?
Day 4: Own the Problem: Walk it to the solution. If a problem reaches you, own it until solved or physically and verbally transferred to a named person who accepts it. Example: Do not point toward a server; introduce the owner, state the request, receive confirmation, and follow up.
Five-minute drill - Own the Problem: When is a handoff actually complete? Practice a warm transfer for three common requests.. Proof: Named owner; Confirmation and follow-up
Today''s floor challenge: Walk every problem to a person or solution.. Proof: Named owner; Confirmation and follow-up; Close with: Which problem stayed yours until the guest felt closure?
Day 5: Ticket-Time Recovery: Update before frustration spikes. Find aging tickets early, give an accurate update, and keep the table cared for while the item moves. Example: You have waited longer than you should have. I am checking the exact status and will return in two minutes.
Five-minute drill - Ticket-Time Recovery: When should a guest hear about a delay? Role-play a 30-minute delay with two proactive updates.. Proof: Accuracy and timing; No vague promises
Today''s floor challenge: Find one aging ticket early and update before being chased.. Proof: Accuracy and timing; No vague promises; Close with: Where did an update prevent frustration?
Day 6: Incorrect Entrée: Repair the whole table experience. Own the missed item, protect food safety, involve the MOD, and care for the rest of the table while the correction moves. Example: Acknowledge the error, remove or manage the item appropriately, set a realistic update, and follow up with everyone affected.
Five-minute drill - Incorrect Entrée: Who needs care when one plate is wrong? Run a wrong-item recovery with one guest waiting while others have food.. Proof: No blame; Rest of table is not forgotten
Today''s floor challenge: Manage both the correction and the table''s pace.. Proof: No blame; Rest of table is not forgotten; Close with: Did we repair the table, not only the plate?
Day 7: They Simply Do Not Like It: Separate preference from preparation. Do not debate taste. Ask what missed - sweetness, spice, texture, or style - then guide the replacement from the answer. Example: I am sorry this is not landing for you. Is it the sweetness, spice, or overall style?
Five-minute drill - They Simply Do Not Like It: Can a correctly made item still be wrong for this guest? Recover without insulting the dish or making the guest wrong.. Proof: No defensiveness; Replacement fits new information
Today''s floor challenge: Ask one preference question before a replacement recommendation.. Proof: No defensiveness; Replacement fits new information; Close with: Was the issue preparation, expectation, or preference?
Day 8: The Recovery Follow-Up: Verify the emotion, not only the transaction. Operational action does not prove trust is repaired. Return personally and ask whether the solution actually worked. Example: I wanted to come back personally and make sure the replacement is right and that we have truly taken care of this.
Five-minute drill - The Recovery Follow-Up: Why is removing an item from the check not the end? Practice a ten-second follow-up specific to the original concern.. Proof: Specific, personal follow-up; Arrives in time to fix another miss
Today''s floor challenge: Follow up on every recovery before departure.. Proof: Specific, personal follow-up; Arrives in time to fix another miss; Close with: Which follow-up proved whether recovery worked?
Day 9: Think Beyond the Complaint: Find the system behind the miss. After care is restored, capture the standard, owner, trigger, method, verification, and recovery that failed. Example: A missed allergy communication requires a full system review, not a reminder to one person.
Five-minute drill - Think Beyond the Complaint: If this happened tomorrow, what would we wish we changed tonight? Run the six-part System Test on a recent complaint.. Proof: Root cause is specific; Action is more than ''remind everyone''
Today''s floor challenge: Record one system lesson from a recovery.. Proof: Root cause is specific; Action is more than ''remind everyone''; Close with: What will change so tomorrow''s team is less likely to repeat it?
Day 10: Full L.A.S.T. Certification: Combine care and learning. The structure lives in the teammate''s thinking while the language stays natural: Listen, Apologize, Solve, Think. Example: Complete the full model without announcing the acronym''s letters.
Five-minute drill - Full L.A.S.T. Certification: Can we stay calm, human, and decisive from first sentence through follow-up? Run a surprise 90-second scenario and explain the Think step.. Proof: Listening; Acknowledgment; Ownership; Follow-up; System thinking
Today''s floor challenge: Use the full model on any real recovery and log evidence.. Proof: Listening; Acknowledgment; Ownership; Follow-up; System thinking; Close with: Which L.A.S.T. step needs another cycle?', ARRAY['curriculum', 'daily-mastermind', 'cycle-3', 'leadership', 'floor-training', 'practical-required']::text[]),
  ('search-cur-mastermind-accountability-without-ego', 'cur-mastermind-accountability-without-ego', 'module', 'Cycle 4 - Accountability Without Ego', 'Direct feedback that protects people and standards. Ten shifts, one five-minute behavior rep per shift.
Outcomes: Run all ten pre-shift sessions in sequence.; Observe and coach each day''s behavior during live service.; Repeat a day when behavior has not transferred to the floor.
Cycle standard: Coach observable behavior, receive with Heard, create the next rep, and verify the reset without humiliation or theater.
Day 1: Feedback Is Not Disrespect: Separate correction from attack. Discomfort is not disrespect. Healthy accountability names behavior, connects it to a shared standard, and requests a reset. Example: ''Your station has been below standard twice'' is coachable; ''You are lazy'' attacks identity.
Five-minute drill - Feedback Is Not Disrespect: Why can respectful correction still feel uncomfortable? Convert two labels into neutral observations and clear actions.. Proof: Tone, privacy, specificity; Employee knows what to change
Today''s floor challenge: Give one timely behavioral correction without apology or aggression.. Proof: Tone, privacy, specificity; Employee knows what to change; Close with: Did feedback protect the standard and dignity?
Day 2: The ''Heard'' Response: Receive before explaining. Heard signals the standard and action were received. Fix first; give operationally useful context after the reset. Example: Heard. Fixed. Can you show me what you want next time so I hit it consistently?
Five-minute drill - The ''Heard'' Response: What becomes possible when the first response is Heard? Receive, fix, then ask one useful clarification without defense.. Proof: No counter-accusation or résumé speech; Clarification serves execution
Today''s floor challenge: Use Heard as the first response to every valid correction.. Proof: No counter-accusation or résumé speech; Clarification serves execution; Close with: Where did clean receiving save time or tension?
Day 3: 10 for 10: Reset drift before it compounds. 10 for 10 is a neutral, roughly 30-second return to standard. Anyone can call it; leaders participate and verify. Example: 10 for 10, bar. Glassware home, trash clear, garnish full - beautiful, that is our standard.
Five-minute drill - 10 for 10: What small disorder could become the next bottleneck? Create three station misses and complete the reset without blame.. Proof: Timely, shared, complete; Released without lingering emotion
Today''s floor challenge: Use one appropriate reset only if the room actually drifts.. Proof: Timely, shared, complete; Released without lingering emotion; Close with: Did the reset protect the next hour?
Day 4: Keep / Start / Stop: Make coaching balanced and actionable. Keep reinforces behavior worth repeating; Start names a missing action; Stop names behavior creating friction. Never invent praise. Example: Keep your table energy. Start checking the pass. Stop walking past food because it is not yours.
Five-minute drill - Keep / Start / Stop: What should continue, begin, and end? Give a 30-second Keep / Start / Stop for a food-running miss.. Proof: Behavior, not personality; One clear action per category
Today''s floor challenge: Use the framework once and confirm the reset.. Proof: Behavior, not personality; One clear action per category; Close with: What is now easier to repeat or change?
Day 5: Behavior, Not Identity: Remove labels from coaching. Coach what can be observed and changed. Identity labels create shame and argument; behavior creates a path forward. Example: You argued before acknowledging two corrections is usable; your attitude sucks is not.
Five-minute drill - Behavior, Not Identity: Can a person act carelessly without being a careless person? Rewrite five labels as observation, standard, and next action.. Proof: No always, never, lazy, careless; Evidence is quotable
Today''s floor challenge: Catch every personality label before it leaves your mouth.. Proof: No always, never, lazy, careless; Evidence is quotable; Close with: What exact behavior needs to change?
Day 6: Challenge Up Respectfully: Protect the standard across titles. Challenge upward with timely facts, appropriate privacy, and the shared outcome. Leaders reward the courage they request. Example: I may be missing context, but the allergy item moved without verification. Can we pause and confirm?
Five-minute drill - Challenge Up Respectfully: How can someone challenge a leader without a power contest? Team member challenges a manager; manager receives with Heard. Thank you.. Proof: Safety, facts, timing, tone; No retaliation or dismissal
Today''s floor challenge: Raise one legitimate concern directly to the correct person.. Proof: Safety, facts, timing, tone; No retaliation or dismissal; Close with: How did leadership respond when the standard moved upward?
Day 7: Coach a Peer: Share ownership without acting like a boss. Peer accountability is brief, factual, and anchored in the shared standard. Transfer formal issues rather than escalating conflict. Example: We have food sitting and both of us are clear. Can you grab 31 while I take 28?
Five-minute drill - Coach a Peer: What makes peer coaching helpful instead of controlling? Practice one peer reset and one escalation after refusal.. Proof: No bossy tone or embarrassment; Formal conflict transfers appropriately
Today''s floor challenge: Protect one standard with clean peer language.. Proof: No bossy tone or embarrassment; Formal conflict transfers appropriately; Close with: Where did peer accountability help without drama?
Day 8: Receive Feedback From Anyone: Let the best information win. Titles do not create immunity. Thank the person, check the standard, correct, and follow up. Example: A new host catches an expired restroom check; the manager says Heard, fixes it, and recognizes the catch.
Five-minute drill - Receive Feedback From Anyone: What does a junior employee learn when a leader gets defensive? The junior person gives a correction; the leader practices receiving and acting.. Proof: No power signals or dismissal; Behavior actually changes
Today''s floor challenge: Invite one piece of upward feedback and do not explain first.. Proof: No power signals or dismissal; Behavior actually changes; Close with: What did someone see that leadership needed to hear?
Day 9: Correct Without Embarrassment: Choose the right audience and moment. Correct risk immediately; protect dignity. A short public safety cue may be necessary, while deeper coaching usually belongs in private. Example: At the pass: Allergy hold - verify now. In private later: observation, standard, risk, and next rep.
Five-minute drill - Correct Without Embarrassment: When should correction be public, and when private? Sort five scenarios into immediate cue, private coach, or formal follow-up.. Proof: No sarcasm or public lecture; Safety corrections are not delayed
Today''s floor challenge: Use the smallest audience that can safely correct the issue.. Proof: No sarcasm or public lecture; Safety corrections are not delayed; Close with: Did correction preserve the standard and ability to recover?
Day 10: Accountability Reflection: Make clean feedback cultural. No-ego culture means feedback travels quickly in every direction and people recover fast. Vocabulary alone is not proof. Example: Keep clean resets. Start recognizing people who receive well. Stop debating before fixing.
Five-minute drill - Accountability Reflection: What accountability behavior should this team keep, start, and stop? Complete a team Keep / Start / Stop about feedback.. Proof: Standards protected; Fear or ego still slowing action
Today''s floor challenge: Demonstrate the cleanest receive-and-reset behavior of the cycle.. Proof: Standards protected; Fear or ego still slowing action; Close with: What will prove two weeks from now that accountability changed?', ARRAY['curriculum', 'daily-mastermind', 'cycle-4', 'leadership', 'floor-training', 'practical-required']::text[]),
  ('search-cur-trainer-identity-selection', 'cur-trainer-identity-selection', 'module', 'Trainer Identity, Selection + Contract', 'Select culture carriers through evidence, not tenure, speed, popularity, or schedule convenience.
Outcomes: Explain the trainer mission and role boundaries; Apply the evidence-based selection standard; Commit to source discipline and honest sign-offs
Trainer mission: We do not hand people information. We build capability. Training is hospitality before the guest ever arrives.
Who earns the responsibility: Role competence under normal volume and pressure; Culture and hospitality; Consistency; Clear communication; Patience without lowered standards; Behavioral accountability; Reliability and source discipline
Trainer vs. leadership: Prepare the objective and current source -> Selection, schedules, materials, bandwidth, and system health; Demonstrate, create reps, coach, and document evidence -> Policy, discipline, accommodations, employment decisions, and final certification authority; Build independence -> Protect enough training bandwidth and resolve source conflicts
